# Changelog (engine/v1/)

## Simulator loads are re-seated onto the platform

- **What:** `Simulator.Load` now centres the loaded object on the disc in X/Z. The obstacle branch's existing Y snap gained matching `deltaX`/`deltaZ` terms derived from the platform mesh's position and the object's world AABB centre; a new `else` branch does the entity equivalent by writing the platform's X/Z straight onto `built.transform.position` and calling `UpdateEntityModelFromTransform`, which re-poses the model and recomputes the collision AABB that the framing step reads immediately after.
- **Why:** The Simulator neutralized the *surface reference* for entities (`mergeSimulatorEntity` forces `spawnSurfaceId: "sim-disc"`) but nothing ever neutralized the *placement offset*, so a loaded object kept its authored level position while the disc sits at `{10, 0, 10}`. Terrain and obstacles used their absolute `position` verbatim — testGame's `smoke-torch` at `{19, 1, 5}` landed 9 cnu off in X and 5 off in Z. Entities were displaced by a different route: a blueprint's `rootTransform.position` is `{0,0,0}`, but `BuildEntity` teleports the root onto the movement path whenever `movement.speed > 0` ([NewEntity.js:560-562](engine/v1/builder/NewEntity.js#L560)), so surface-local `start`/`end` carried the model off the disc. Pre-existing since the Simulator gained obstacle/terrain support; the entity half only became reachable once a Simulator-loadable entity was given a movement path. Fixed in `Simulator.Load` rather than at the normalize boundary because the two entity merge paths produce different shapes — `mergeTemplateRefEntity` ([normalize.js:969](engine/v1/core/normalize.js#L969)) emits no `movement` key at all and permits a null `rootTransform.position`, so neutralizing placement there would have needed a shape guard, while the built object is uniform.
- **Where:** `handlers/game/Simulator.js:13,268-290` (`UpdateEntityModelFromTransform` import, the shared `platformMesh` binding, the extended obstacle snap and the new entity branch).

## Particle generators in the Simulator

- **What:** Level-authored generators now build and tick in the Simulator. Two independent gaps each individually sufficient to produce nothing were closed: the build-time sweep that turns an authored `particle` field into a group was inline in `CreateLevel`, and `Simulator.Load` builds through `SpawnIntoScene`, which never ran it; and `Update`'s simulator branch returned before `updateParticles`. `NewParticles.js` gained exported `ParticleGeneratorRequests(carrier, kind)` holding the carrier→request mapping (terrain mesh / obstacle record / entity part) moved out of `NewLevel.js`, so the level build and the Simulator produce identical target keys and origin math from one function. `Level.js` gained `updateParticles` in the simulator branch and an exported `SpawnParticleRequests(requests, sceneGraph)` that generates prepared requests into a live scene. `Simulator.Load` builds the requests, forces `physics: "none"` on each, and calls it; a new `clearParticles` local runs in `Load` and `Clear`. Supporting changes: a module-scoped `overrideKeys` array is now the sole owner of the six overridable particle keys, `resolveRequest` loops it instead of six explicit picks, a new exported `WithParticleOverride(overrides, key, value)` returns a complete six-key set with one key replaced, and a `generateForScene(request, viewerPosition, sceneGraph)` helper collapses the five-argument `GenerateParticles` call shared by `SpawnParticles`, `SpawnParticleRequests` and the sibling-reseed loop.
- **Why:** The one place built for previewing a model could not preview the effect attached to it. Three points are load-bearing. The Simulator runs no physics pipeline, so `"simple"`/`"full"` groups would emit and never translate — forcing `"none"` routes all motion through `advance()` instead of special-casing the frame. That override could not be expressed as a partial `{ ...overrides, physics: "none" }` spread, because `normalize` guarantees `overrides` is null-or-complete and a partial object would have forced `resolveRequest` to tolerate `undefined` with `??` — a `FORBIDDEN_DEFENSIVE_CHECKS` §1 violation whose shape is §3B's own bad example; hence `WithParticleOverride` returning a complete set, so no downstream tolerance is added anywhere. And stale groups are removed explicitly rather than left to phase 4's auto-orphaning, because reloading the *same* id is the core Simulator workflow and is the one case orphaning cannot cover: the old groups find a live target under an identical `type::id::partId` key, never orphan, and keep seeding alongside the new object's groups, compounding emitters on every reload. Live target tracking needed no Simulator-specific handling at all — the loaded object is stationary and is its own target, so `follow()` resolves to an unchanging transform every frame.
- **Where:** `builder/NewParticles.js:102-118,399-414` (`overrideKeys`, rewritten `resolveRequest`, `WithParticleOverride`, `ParticleGeneratorRequests`); `builder/NewLevel.js:366-380` (three `ParticleGeneratorRequests` calls replacing the inline `generatorSources` list; the `RotateByEuler` import became unused and was removed); `handlers/game/Level.js:306-309,334,351,556,574-582` (`generateForScene`, the simulator-branch tick, `SpawnParticleRequests`); `handlers/game/Simulator.js:6,215-216,249,296-303,322` (imports, `clearParticles` and its two call sites, the `Load` sweep).

## Particle system — live target tracking + entity generators

- **What:** Particle generators now resolve their spawn origin per frame against a live scene target instead of freezing it at build/request time, and entity parts can carry a generator. A group stores `target`/`targetKey`/`offset`/`originCorrection` and gains `follow(targets)`/`orphan()`; a target that disappears orphans the group, which then stops recycling/seeding and despawns after its authored decay. Targets are `{ type, id, partId }`, keyed by a single non-exported `particleTargetKey` in `NewParticles.js`, and resolved once per frame by `resolveGeneratorTargets` in `Level.js` (a map rebuilt from the live arrays, so it cannot desync). Generator origins are now offset-rotated by the target's rotation. Root-level `particle` on an object that has `parts` is now warn-and-dropped at normalize — placement is per-part only once parts exist.
- **Why:** Phases 1–3 froze the spawn position, which was only correct while terrain and obstacle parts were the sole carriers. Entity generators, moving carriers and dead targets all needed the origin to be live; entity `particle` was authored, validated and normalized but silently discarded at the builder. The multi-part root placement was accepted by schema and normalize and then dropped by the builder in silence.
- **Where:** `builder/NewParticles.js:220-265,309-313,327` (`particleTargetKey`, constructor fields, `follow`/`orphan`, `expire`, `seedDue`); `builder/NewEntity.js:191` (`particle` on the entity-part `BuildObject` source); `builder/NewLevel.js:366-392` (three generator sources materialized before the loop, rotated offsets, `target`); `handlers/game/Level.js:310-316,417-479,545-554` (`resolveGeneratorTargets`, `indexById`, `targetTransform`, `findSceneTarget(id, partId)` now returning `{ type, transform }`, `SpawnParticles` offset/rotation); `core/normalize.js:776-783,818,869-870,1278-1290` (`normalizeRootParticle` and its two call sites — note `object.parts` is now populated *before* the root-particle call, since the check needs the part count; request `offset`/`target` slots); `core/validate.js:351,378-383` (`partId` passthrough, missing-part error); `core/canonSchemas.json:2097` (`particleRequest.target.partId`).

  **Placement rule established this pass:** an object with a `parts` array accepts `particle` per part only, never at the root; an object with no parts keeps its root field because there is nowhere else for it to go. Entities always have parts, so entity generators are per-part only and there is deliberately no entity-root `particle` field. The discriminator is `parts.length > 0`, matching the builders' own `source.parts.length === 0` branch ([NewObstacle.js:77](engine/v1/builder/NewObstacle.js#L77), [NewTerrain.js:63](engine/v1/builder/NewTerrain.js#L63)).

## Particles no longer collide with entities + `max` spread tier

- **What:** `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` ([Collision.js:402](engine/v1/physics/Collision.js#L402)) gained a fourth parameter `includeEntities`; its entity-collection block is now wrapped in it, and both call sites — `DetectPhysicsCollisions` ([:669](engine/v1/physics/Collision.js#L669)) and `DetectCurrentPhysicsOverlaps` ([:774](engine/v1/physics/Collision.js#L774)) — pass `entity.type !== "particle"`. A particle now collects terrain, obstacles and void walls only. Separately, `spreadHalfAngles` ([NewParticles.js:44](engine/v1/builder/NewParticles.js#L44)) gained a fourth tier `max` at a 180° half-angle — a full sphere, not a cone — and `"max"` was added to the `spread` `allowedValues` in all six copies of the particle overrides block in `canonSchemas.json`. `randomInCone` needed no change: `theta = acos(randomRange(cos(π), 1))` is already correct uniform sphere sampling at that half-angle. testGame's spark burst now sends `overrides: { count: 40, spread: "max" }`.
- **Why:** Sparks requested on the `cnu-fall-cube`'s landing were invisible, and the cause was that they were born inside solid geometry and had their launch velocity deleted on the first frame. `physics/Master.js` sends `position: CloneVector3(entity.transform.position)` in the `ENTITY_COLLISION` payload — the emitter's *own transform origin*, not the contact point — which for that 1×1×1 cube is its bottom face, resting on the floor. `GenerateParticles` then re-centers each group's AABB on that y, so every 0.06 cnu spark spawned half-buried in terrain and flush against the cube's underside. `physics: "full"` sets `collision: true`, and the pre-existing particle skip in the broadphase only stopped a particle from *being* an obstruction — the spark is the entity being simulated, so it still collected the cube as a solid. `ResolveCollisions` strips the velocity component along every contact normal when `velDotN < 0`; the cube's underside normal points down against a +4.5 y launch, so the whole launch was removed, three overlap iterations per frame. The ballistics were never at fault (4.5 against `Gravity.Strength` 10 cnu/s² apexes at 1.01 cnu after 450 ms, inside the 700 ms life; air drag at `10/30 = 0.33/s` is negligible). The **terrain** half needed no fix and was deliberately left alone: a ground normal points up, so an upward-moving particle has `velDotN > 0`, keeps its velocity, and is ejected upward by `pushDepth`. Gating inside the broadphase rather than filtering in the consumer loops also skips the entity walk entirely for particles, which matters because it runs once in the swept pass plus three times in the overlap loop, per particle, per frame. The `max` tier answers a separate symptom of the same report: `randomInCone` is sampled **once per group**, so 20 sparks in a 10° cone resolved to only four near-identical trajectories. Per-particle velocity is **rejected, permanently and by design** — parts within a group must stay a rigid formation — so an all-directions burst is instead expressed as a wider cone plus a higher count (40 yields 8–20 groups, so 8–20 distinct trajectories over a full sphere).
- **Where:** `physics/Collision.js:402,484-498,669,774`; `builder/NewParticles.js:44-49`; `core/canonSchemas.json` (six `spread.__meta.allowedValues` arrays); `testGame/main.js:400`.

## Particle system — game-facing API + runtime-request validate/normalize boundary

- **What:** Added `ENGINE.Level.SpawnParticles(request, generator)` and, behind it, a new centralized boundary for *small runtime requests* from the running game — distinct from the full-payload boundary. `core/normalize.js` gained a `Runtime` object (exported as a key on the default export) and `core/validate.js` gained an exported `ValidateRuntime`; both carry a `Particles` entry plus a `helpers` sub-object for internals shared across future request types. The contract is validate → normalize (invoked *by* validate) → build → insert, fire-and-forget with no return value. New `particleRequest` root in `canonSchemas.json` (`id` aliased to `templateId`, `position`, `overrides`, `target`); `position` is absolute in burst mode and an offset from `target` in generator mode. `NewParticles.js` replaced `request.seeding: boolean` with `request.mode: "generator" | "sibling" | "burst"`, added `expire()` as an overridable base method (default `recycle()`), a `burstParticleGroup` subclass that sets `finished = true` instead, a `lifespan` field (base `duration.intervalMs`, overridden by the burst subclass to `duration.endTimeMs`), and per-group `targetId` storage for a future follow-the-target pass. `handlers/game/Level.js` gained `findSceneTarget(sceneGraph, id)` (a short-circuiting lookup returning a flat `{ type, position }` or `undefined`) and `SpawnParticles`, and `updateParticles` now splices finished groups inside its reverse-index loop. `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE.Particles` is `"High"` and `CONFIG.CUSTOM_EVENTS.Entities.collision` is `true`.
- **Why:** `GenerateParticles` was engine-internal with two engine-side call sites, so a game could not request an effect at all. The request-boundary pair was built as a shared ecosystem rather than a standalone `ValidateParticlePayload` because future runtime request types each need their own validate/normalize pair. Three points are load-bearing. Normalization walks the schema first and `structuredClone`s the *result*, which keeps unclonable values (a callback on the request would throw `DataCloneError`) out of the clone because the walker drops unknown keys first, and stays robust if a future schema node is added without full decomposition. The scene reaches the validator as a passed-in `id → { type, position }` **resolver function**, because `core/` cannot import `handlers/` and passing `sceneGraph` would drag builder-output contracts into `core/`; a resolver also defers the lookup until the schema has proven there is an id to look up. And the API path registers textures because a level with no level-authored generator has no `"none"` entry in `textureRegistry` and `Render.js` dereferences it unguarded — particle parts use role `"entity-part"`, never take the face-texture path, and always carry the literal `"none"` texture id, which only reaches the registry at build time because `NewLevel.js` pushes groups into `entities` before `PrepareLevelVisualResources` runs. `AddToVisualResources` must be passed `"entity"`, never `"particle"`: `ENTITY_TYPES` has no `"particle"` and the fallback branch dereferences `built.dimensions` on an entity record.
- **Where:** `core/canonSchemas.json` (new `particleRequest` root at the end of the file); `core/normalize.js` (`Runtime` section above the default export); `core/validate.js` (`/* === RUNTIME REQUESTS === */` section above the export block); `builder/NewParticles.js` (constructor fields, `expire`, `lifespan`, `burstParticleGroup`, three-way class select in `GenerateParticles`); `builder/NewLevel.js` (build-time request now `mode: "generator"`, `target: null`); `handlers/game/Level.js` (`findSceneTarget`, `SpawnParticles`, splice in `updateParticles`); `core/ini.js` (`Level.SpawnParticles`); `core/config.js`; `testGame/main.js` (`handleEntityCollision` spark burst).

## Particle system — runtime tick, physicsMode authority, self-seeding

- **What:** Particles now age, move, decay and sustain themselves. `physicsMode` became the **sole authority** over a particle's five physics stage flags: `physicsFlags` was deleted from both `particles.json` templates and from `resolveRequest`, replaced by `physicsFlagsFor(mode)` ([NewParticles.js:123](engine/v1/builder/NewParticles.js#L123)) deriving `"none"` → all five false, `"simple"` → collision only, `"full"` → gravity + resistance + collision. Two non-exported classes replace the old plain `entity.particle` object: `particleGroup` ([NewParticles.js:232](engine/v1/builder/NewParticles.js#L232)) carrying frame-tracked lifetime, decay and recycle, and `seedingParticleGroup` ([NewParticles.js:301](engine/v1/builder/NewParticles.js#L301)) adding staggered self-seeding. Lifetime is numerical — `total` accumulates `deltaMs` and phase derives from it against `duration.holdTimeMs`/`endTimeMs`, with no wall clock and no `setInterval`. Decay always resolves from a stored base (`displayColor.a = baseAlpha * decay`), never multiplied. At `total >= duration.intervalMs` a group **recycles rather than despawning**: it resets `total`/`decayStart`, teleports back to a stored `spawnOrigin`, resets scale, re-randomizes velocity via `entity.velocity.set(...)`, clears `physicsRuntime.lastPhysicsCollisionKey`, and re-randomizes per-part colour in place. A new authored template field `amount` is the number of concurrent phase-offset emissions; only the first group of a level-authored request is a `seedingParticleGroup`, emitting `amount - 1` siblings at `intervalMs / amount` stride on a monotonic `seedClock` that recycle does not reset. `seedDue()` is a base-class method returning `false`, overridden by the subclass, so the driver calls a guaranteed method rather than probing for one. A new `updateParticles(sceneGraph, deltaMilliseconds, deltaSeconds)` ([Level.js:308](engine/v1/handlers/game/Level.js#L308)) runs once per frame between the entity pass and the camera block, using a reverse-index loop because seeding appends to the array it walks; siblings are pushed straight into `sceneGraph.entities`. A `physicsMode === "none"` skip in the entity pass lets those particles bypass `ApplyPhysicsPipeline` entirely. `BuildEntity` now sets `particle: null` ([NewEntity.js:601](engine/v1/builder/NewEntity.js#L601)) so the field is guaranteed on every entity. `GenerateParticles` gained a `viewerPosition` parameter and two generation gates: a `CONFIG.PERFORMANCE.Particles` tier (High → 1, Medium → 0.5 applied to `count`, Low → 0 plus a warn) and a simulation-distance check. `physics/Collision.js` gained an exported `IsBeyondSimDistance(viewerPosition, targetPosition)` ([:375](engine/v1/physics/Collision.js#L375)) now used by `NewParticles.js`, `Level.js` and `Collectible.js`. New `smoke` template plus a `smoke-torch` obstacle in testGame level 1 stage 2.
- **Why:** Phase 1 built particle groups but nothing aged them — `entity.particle.duration` and `.physicsMode` were written and read by nothing, so a generator emitted one frozen batch. Three points are load-bearing and non-obvious. The `"simple"` mode contradiction (a true collision flag *and* motion "not from the physics pipeline") resolves as **the tick owns the arc, the pipeline owns integration**: `"simple"` decrements `entity.velocity.y` by `arcRate` each frame while `ApplyPhysicsPipeline` still integrates position and resolves collision, so only `"none"` bypasses the pipeline and no flag becomes dead data. Shrink goes through `entity.transform.scale` rather than per-part `localScale` — one write instead of five, and since `ComposeTransform` does not scale local positions by parent scale, each particle shrinks in place while the group's spread holds. `count` and `amount` are orthogonal: `count` sizes one emission (already split across several in-phase groups because `groupLayouts` caps at 5 parts per entity) while `amount` is how many phase-offset emissions are alive at once — continuity comes from spacing *between* emissions, never within one. Deleting `physicsFlags` also fixed a live bug: `overrides.physics: "full"` previously changed `resolved.physics` while `physicsFlags` stayed template-owned, producing a "full" particle with every flag false so gravity never applied.
- **Where:** `builder/NewParticles.js` (243→370: `physicsFlagsFor`, `arcRate`, `shrinkFloor`, `performanceParticleMultiplier`, `randomizeColor`, both classes, the two gates, new `viewerPosition` parameter); `builder/templates/particles.json` (`physicsFlags` removed, `amount` added, new `smoke` entry); `builder/NewEntity.js:601`; `builder/NewLevel.js` (`seeding: true` + build-time viewer position); `handlers/game/Level.js:306-329,359-363,370` (`updateParticles`, the `"none"` skip, the call site); `physics/Collision.js:375,1098` (`IsBeyondSimDistance`); `handlers/game/Collectible.js:25` and `handlers/game/Level.js:363` (routed onto the shared helper); `testGame/levels/levels.json` (`smoke-torch`).

## Collision physics stage flag (entity opt-out)

- **What:** Added a fifth per-entity physics stage flag, `collision` (fallback `true`), alongside the existing `gravity`/`buoyancy`/`resistance`/`correction` on `movement.physics` (non-player) and `character.physics` (player). `physics/Collision.js` gained an exported `GetEntityPhysicsFlags(entity)` helper (`entity.type === "player" ? entity.character.physics : entity.movement.physics`), used both by its own new gate — `DetectPhysicsCollisions`/`DetectCurrentPhysicsOverlaps`'s existing `CONFIG.PHYSICS.Collision.Enabled === false` early-return now also fires on `!GetEntityPhysicsFlags(entity).collision` — and by `physics/Master.js`, whose two prior independent copies of the same ternary were replaced with calls to the shared helper. `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` gained an `includeTriggers` parameter so trigger candidates are only collected for the player, since non-player entities never had their trigger-overlap results consumed by anything (`storePlayerTriggers` in `physics/Master.js` is the sole reader and was already player-gated) — this was pure discarded work before. `core/canonSchemas.json`'s `levelMovement.physics` schema, `player/characters.json` (3 templates), `builder/templates/characters.json` (`template-chara`), and `builder/templates/particles.json` (`dust: false`, `spark: true`) all carry the new key.
- **Why:** `builder/NewParticles.js` particles are ordinary entities in `sceneGraph.entities`, and `handlers/game/Level.js` calls `ApplyPhysicsPipeline` on every entity unconditionally — none of the four pre-existing physics flags gated collision detection/resolution, so an "inert" particle template (all four `false`, e.g. `dust`) still ran full swept-collision and overlap-resolution every frame for no purpose. The new flag closes that gap generically, for any entity, not just particles.
- **Where:** `core/canonSchemas.json` (`levelMovement.physics.collision`); `player/characters.json`, `builder/templates/characters.json`, `builder/templates/particles.json` (data); `physics/Collision.js` (`GetEntityPhysicsFlags`, both detect functions' gate + `BroadphaseCollectCandidates`'s `includeTriggers`); `physics/Master.js:72,172` (consolidated onto the shared helper).

## Particle system — core generation infrastructure

- **What:** Added the generation half of the particle system. New `builder/templates/particles.json` is an engine-owned template collection (two authored types, `dust` and `spark`) carrying `count`, `spread`, `physics`, `color.min`/`max`, `velocity`, `duration` (`mode` `"fade"`/`"shrink"`, `holdTimeMs`, `endTimeMs`, `intervalMs`), `physicsFlags`, and a single-part prototype; it is exposed as `ENGINE.Blueprints.Particles` through `Instance.js`'s existing raw/instanced split, so `ini.js` needed no change. New `builder/NewParticles.js` exports `GenerateParticles(request, textureScale, faceTextureStore, geometryCache) → { groups }`, which resolves a template plus per-call overrides and builds each group as an ordinary multi-part entity via `BuildEntity` (`entity.type === "particle"`, plus an `entity.particle` block holding `templateId`/`duration`/`physicsMode`/`velocity`). Group sizing is tiered: `count < 3` yields N single-part groups, `3–7` yields three near-equal groups, and `>= 8` samples a group count with every group 2–5 parts (sampled rather than enumerated — partitions of N into {2,3,4,5} grow ≈ N⁴/2880). Per-particle randomized colour is written to `mesh.displayColor`; per-group velocity is the template vector scaled 0.8–1.2× and perturbed inside a spread cone (10°/30°/60°). Level payloads gained a persistent-generator surface: any `levelPart` or `levelObject` (and their `templateObjectRef`/`templateEntityRef` equivalents) may carry `particle: { id, position, overrides }`, threaded into `mesh.meta` by `NewObject.js`/`NewObstacle.js` and swept at build time by `NewLevel.js`, which pushes the generated groups into `sceneGraph.entities`. `normalize.js` gained a shared `normalizeParticle` helper that validates `particle.id` against the new template collection (warn-and-drop) and instances `particle.position` plus `overrides.velocity`; `overrides` is restricted to six enumerated keys (`count`, `spread`, `physics`, `color`, `velocity`, `duration`) with all nested children declared. `NewTemplate.js`'s `cloneTemplatePart` was renamed and exported as `CloneTemplatePart` and gained a `cloneParticle` helper so repeat-expanded parts never alias one generator instance; `Instance.js` canonicalizes an absent `part.particle` to `null` at boot, mirroring how `instancePartRepeat` already handles `repeat`. `Collision.js` skips particles in both `BroadphaseCollectCandidates` and `DetectCombatOverlaps`.
- **Why:** `Particles` was a standing priority in `docs/todo` with no code behind it. This lands generation end-to-end so a game can author persistent generators through the ordinary payload boundary, while deliberately leaving the per-frame tick out of scope. Three design points are load-bearing and non-obvious: per-particle colour must ride `mesh.displayColor` rather than `texture.generated.primary`, because `ComputeGeneratedTextureID` hashes `primary`/`secondary` into the texture id and would bake a unique canvas per particle (the template's texture is `"none"`, the only pure-white entry, since the tint uniform is multiplicative); `entity.velocity` is non-zero only in physics mode `"full"`, because `ApplyPhysicsPipeline` computes displacement outside every physics-flag check, so zeroed flags alone do not make the pipeline inert; and velocity is per-group rather than per-particle, because the physics pipeline owns exactly one `entity.velocity` per entity and a group is an entity. Groups live in `sceneGraph.entities` rather than a separate array specifically so `"full"` mode can reach the real physics loops. `"particle"` was deliberately **not** added to `ENTITY_TYPES` — that array selects payload schemas and particles are never a payload entity type — while `entity.type === "particle"` remains load-bearing as a runtime discriminator.
- **Where:** New `builder/NewParticles.js` and `builder/templates/particles.json`; `builder/templates/Instance.js:11,41,117-124,142,160` (`instanceParticleTemplates`, absent-`particle` canonicalization, `Particles` in the raw/instanced maps); `builder/NewTemplate.js:47-56,69,91,159` (`cloneParticle`, `CloneTemplatePart` export rename, `particle` forwarding in `ResolveObjectSource`/`applyPartOverride`); `core/canonSchemas.json` (`particle` node on `levelPart`, `levelObject`, `templateObjectRef` root and `parts.__entry`, and `templateEntityRef.model.parts.__entry`); `core/normalize.js:17,743,763-774,791,809,844,860` plus `particle` rehydration in `mergeBlueprintWithOverride`; `builder/NewObject.js:1091,1110,1162,1240` (`particle` in `mesh.meta`); `builder/NewObstacle.js:99` (the single-part path builds an explicit `BuildObject` literal, so it needed threading too); `builder/NewLevel.js:7,12,366-384` (build sweep + `Level`-channel group log); `physics/Collision.js:476,835`.

## Simulator obstacle/terrain support + AABB-derived platform/camera framing

- **What:** Added Obstacle and Terrain model previewing to the Engine Simulator, and made the preview disc platform footprint and DefaultCam framing (`distance` + `heightOffset`) derive from the loaded object's AABB, with the template's hardcoded disc size / camera distance / heightOffset treated as minimums (`Math.max`). Terrain now routes through `BuildTerrain` (role `"terrain"`) in `SpawnIntoScene` instead of the untagged `BuildObject` fallback; `DespawnFromScene` handles multi-part terrain (array targets) and dispatches on `objectType`. `SimulatorPayload` now resolves terrain template refs (via `normalizeObjectTemplateRef` with a `"terrain"`/`"obstacle"` collection selector) instead of dropping them. `Camera.js` gained an exported `SetDefaultCamFraming({distance, heightOffset})` so the simulator retargets framing at runtime without reaching into camera internals. `Simulator.js` `Load()` was reworked with `spawnPlatform()`/`frameLoadedObject()` helpers, `platformMesh` runtime tracking (invariantly an array), a `followTarget` recomputed from the loaded object's AABB center each load, and a `builtObject` that may be an array for multi-part terrain. `NewObstacle.js` exported its AABB-union helper as `MergeAabb` for reuse. `SpawnIntoScene`'s repeated post-spawn tail was collapsed into a `finalizeSpawn` helper. Also renamed pre-existing non-exported `FULL_CAPS` constants to `lowerCamelCase` in `math/Forces.js` (`airDragCoefficient`, `waterDragCoefficient`) and `handlers/game/Enemy.js` (`knockbackForce`, `invulnerabilityDuration`) per CASING.md.
- **Why:** The simulator only previewed entities/players cleanly — terrain hit an untagged-fallback bug and terrain template refs were discarded, while the platform and camera were fixed-size regardless of the loaded object. The `finalizeSpawn` extraction, `MergeAabb`/constant casing, and `objectType`-based despawn dispatch resolve duplication and rule (CASING, discriminator) findings raised by the post-implementation ERA/DRYAD audit.
- **Where:** `core/normalize.js` (`SimulatorPayload` terrain-ref branch); `builder/NewObstacle.js` (`MergeAabb` export); `handlers/game/Camera.js` (`SetDefaultCamFraming`); `handlers/game/Level.js` (`SpawnIntoScene` terrain branch + `finalizeSpawn`, `DespawnFromScene` array/objectType dispatch); `handlers/game/Simulator.js` (`Load` rework, `spawnPlatform`/`frameLoadedObject`, `platformMesh`); `math/Forces.js` and `handlers/game/Enemy.js` (constant casing).
